  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Saturday)\nIssued at 1203 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nSurface high pressure remains anchored over the Great Lakes and\neastern Canada as of this evening, keeping east-northeasterlies\nacross the region. Aloft, a cutoff low currently sits just off\nthe Northeast Coastline with broad, low-amplitude ridging over\nthe desert Southwest. Therefore, the forecast for Sunday is to\nremain cool with elevated, light rain showers and highs in the\n80s. Dry air near the surface, which has been producing afternoon\ndewpoint depressions above 20 F, will very likely evaporate any\nattempts for rain to reach the ground. Additionally, such dry\nconditions will yield afternoon relative humidity values below\n30%, potentially dropping below 20% in portions of northern\nMississippi. This presents above-average fire danger due to a\nlack of widespread, appreciable rainfall during the heat wave\nearlier in September.\n\nThe remnants of Hurricane Polo will phase with a trough in the\nsouthern Rockies through the middle of next week. Lee\ncyclogenesis will kick off ahead of the modified trough over the\nsouthern Plains, which will in turn lead to southerly surface\nwinds returning to the region. A warm up is then expected to take\nplace Tuesday and Wednesday with highs climbing into the upper\n80s and low 90s. Moisture will also increase as well, but diurnal\nmixing is currently expected to keep dewpoints low enough to not\nwarrant any heat index concerns through the middle of the week.\n\nEnsembles have begun to converge on the evolution of the trough,\ngenerally lifting the trough axis northeast into the Midwest\nthrough the end of next week. However, some ensemble members, and\neven a few deterministic runs, show the troughing becoming cutoff\nover the southern Plains. In this scenario, an extended period of\nwarmer, more humid weather would be in store. All of this is to\nsay that the last few days of the forecast period remain mired in\nuncertainty that has drastic effects on our temperature and\nprecipitation forecast, but while the trough remains to our west,\nexpect warmer temperatures and increasing rain chances. By the\nend of the forecast period next Saturday, forecast uncertainty\nexplodes, casting further doubt on the evolution of the upper and\nsurface patterns into early October.",
